WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.420
Film Courage: Have you ever had a filmmaker
submit their film to Hollyshorts, have it

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:11.210
get rejected and then come back to you and
say “You were right, it wasn’t up to screening

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:12.210
level.”

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:15.900
Daniel Sol, Hollyshorts Film Festival Co-Founder:
That really hasn’t happened.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:17.910
Not that I can recall or a direct story, no.

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:20.000
And that’s something that is interesting.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:25.230
There are various ways we can do things to
help encourage people to come out.

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:27.260
Like if people follow it.

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:30.930
If you send rejection letters, people may
not follow up or say anything.

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:34.270
Quite frankly we’re not going to chase them
down and say “Hey you should write back

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:36.500
to me or give me a response to my rejection.”

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:39.260
Obviously we’re not going to chase that
down.

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:41.510
But many people respond with “Hey, why didn’t
I get in?”

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:45.309
Or some people send really nice emails or
constructive criticism emails asking for whatever.

00:00:45.309 --> 00:00:51.199
Some people are angry and ignore that if it’s
really nasty and that type of stuff.

00:00:51.199 --> 00:00:52.430
We get all types of responses.

00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:58.180
We we often see if people say “We’d like
to have a thorough discussion why.

00:00:58.180 --> 00:00:59.559
Why didn’t I get in.”

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:02.629
And we will say Hey, here is a discounted
pass to screenings.

00:01:02.629 --> 00:01:04.540
Come to the festivals, come check out the
films yourself.

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:12.140
And you can compare and contrast and see hey
maybe this is why my film didn’t live up

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:13.770
to these standards and now I understand why.

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:14.770
But that doesn’t happen.

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:19.560
It’s very rare that filmmakers will accept
the pass after that kind of email and actually

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:21.580
come take the pass to the festival.

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:25.930
It does happen some time not very often but
as far as the next step as far as coming up

00:01:25.930 --> 00:01:29.960
to watch the film and actually having a conversation
with us I’ve never had that conversation.

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:33.430
Like just come up and “Hey Daniel I saw
the film and I just want to talk to you.

00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:36.380
I can’t believe you guys offered this pass
to come and check out the films.

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:40.640
It didn’t make it in but I’m here to see
and you know what?

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:45.380
I understand or I see, I learned something
about this or that or whatever the case.

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:48.060
I don’t think my film is up to par, that
doesn’t happen.

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:51.770
Maybe they come in and say “I think my film
is better.”

00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:58.130
Fine but we can have that conversation and
at least they will be at the festival watching

00:01:58.130 --> 00:01:59.690
films, attending, taking part.

00:01:59.690 --> 00:02:04.500
Being accepted or rejected doesn’t mean
you can’t take part in the festival and

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:08.000
buy a ticket and come back.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:11.970
You can buy a ticket for the general public
(the general festival audience) to see films

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:14.819
and see the festival and participate.

00:02:14.819 --> 00:02:16.550
That should happen more often.

00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:20.319
They talk about it as the film school, they
talk about the films that do get in versus

00:02:20.319 --> 00:02:21.319
the films that don’t get in.

00:02:21.319 --> 00:02:25.360
And students getting disappointed by other
students getting in and they don’t get in,

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:26.689
why don’t they come out?

00:02:26.689 --> 00:02:30.099
How come people don’t come out and see the
films as wells and encourage that?

00:02:30.099 --> 00:02:34.400
You should be at the festival and taking the
films in and seeing what might not have worked

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:39.469
for your film.

00:02:39.469 --> 00:02:45.889
That’s important information and something
that should be chased down.

00:02:45.889 --> 00:02:49.470
They should be knocking on our door to attend
the screenings at our festival.

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:52.470
Not “Oh…forget you guys!

00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:53.909
You stink!”

00:02:53.909 --> 00:02:58.200
This idea that we stink and it’s your fault
not mine or something that’s not the right

00:02:58.200 --> 00:02:59.200
mentality.

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:04.749
And that’s what I think is a revealing mentality
is that it’s never anyone else’s fault

00:03:04.749 --> 00:03:06.739
but ours [i.e., festival programmers].

00:03:06.739 --> 00:03:08.579
Not to say that we don’t get things wrong.

00:03:08.579 --> 00:03:14.180
I’m not sitting here trying to toot our
own horn but it’s true that there are probably

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:17.159
many times that there are reasons why the
film didn’t get in and be good.

00:03:17.159 --> 00:03:21.002
It would behoof a filmmaker to say “Let
me go check these films out and see what’s

00:03:21.002 --> 00:03:22.002
going on.

00:03:22.002 --> 00:03:23.940
Let me see what they are looking for.

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:24.980
Let me get a little more information.

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:28.310
They don’t even research all the festivals
to see what they are submitting to.

00:03:28.310 --> 00:03:30.360
It’s just part of the process that you should
know.

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:34.719
It shouldn’t be something that you should
act blindly to like my film is amazing and

00:03:34.719 --> 00:03:38.569
my work is the best and I just believe in
it wholeheartedly and so I don’t care about

00:03:38.569 --> 00:03:42.980
anything else and I should get in and that’s
it because okay I’m right.

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:48.390
Okay well that’s not necessarily real life
so it would be good if people would actually

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:49.529
take initiative that way.

00:03:49.529 --> 00:03:55.200
And I’d be really happy and pleasantly surprised
if that happened but it just doesn’t.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:59.530
Even offering things like pass discounts and
stuff where people encourage to come out and

00:03:59.530 --> 00:04:00.530
check it out.

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:04.980
See what’s what and what other films are
there and maybe what did or didn’t work

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:11.269
or why didn’t they get in, it’s not something
that happens because it should happen more

00:04:11.269 --> 00:04:13.099
often and it doesn’t, it’s just rare.

00:04:13.099 --> 00:04:16.019
Film Courage: Do you accept rough cuts for
submission?

00:04:16.019 --> 00:04:17.340
Daniel: Yes we do (for submissions)?

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:19.150
Film Courage: Do you recommend this?

00:04:19.150 --> 00:04:22.480
Daniel: I mean I don’t necessarily recommend
it, no I don’t.

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:27.759
But I can think of a few off of the top of
my head right now.

00:04:27.759 --> 00:04:30.919
This one film that made it that was a rough
cut that they submitted and it was a giant

00:04:30.919 --> 00:04:34.480
rough cut too because there was a whole element
missing of a big part of the story missing

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:37.840
with visual effects that would have enhanced…they
made it much better when it was done.

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:43.720
But knowing what was there and what was to
come I could see how great the story was and

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:45.590
the potential of what the film was and was
really enjoying.

00:04:45.590 --> 00:04:49.229
I knew that of course I needed to see the
final cut to see what that was.

00:04:49.229 --> 00:04:53.310
If the visual effects were hideous then that
ruins the whole thing but they were great

00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:54.310
and it was really good.

00:04:54.310 --> 00:05:00.990
I had a feeling it was going to be very good
and I loved the movie so it still worked and

00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:01.990
it ended up getting in.

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:05.659
So that was a rough cut and it was a big one
in the sense that a big element of the story

00:05:05.659 --> 00:05:08.240
was simply not even there at that time.

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:13.920
It’s hard to describe without getting into
the details of the story and stuff.

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:15.550
So some rough cuts can still make it in no
problem.

00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:20.759
It’s not some kind of detriment but it can
be if how rough are we talking here?

00:05:20.759 --> 00:05:24.450
Some films are pretty rough cuts where…okay…I
can’t really gauge how good this really

00:05:24.450 --> 00:05:25.889
is?

00:05:25.889 --> 00:05:30.570
As this stands it cannot compete so I guess
what I’m getting is if it’s too much of

00:05:30.570 --> 00:05:34.889
a rough cut or there are still too many elements
missing it probably is best not to submit

00:05:34.889 --> 00:05:40.140
that just for a deadline especially because
look I mean we have out deadlines but oaky

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:41.140
‘Early Bird.’

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:44.509
You might save $10 to $15 or something if
you submit earlier.

00:05:44.509 --> 00:05:47.129
If you submit early you save some money.

00:05:47.129 --> 00:05:50.940
Later you have to pay a little more but I’d
rather pay more money and have a final cut

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:55.879
if I can and have a chance to get accepted
in that way and have my best work forward

00:05:55.879 --> 00:05:58.580
instead of saying well I saved a few bucks
I’m going to submit that deadline.

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:01.000
I’m so eager to get the deadline.

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:03.240
So if I give any advice I’d say look don’t
worry about that.

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:04.470
Spend the extra money.

00:06:04.470 --> 00:06:05.689
We’re not talking about a hundred dollars
here.

00:06:05.689 --> 00:06:08.610
We’re talking about $10 -$15 bucks difference
of a deadline.

00:06:08.610 --> 00:06:13.110
Wait, get it ready and submit the instead
of rushing for that deadline just to submit.

00:06:13.110 --> 00:06:14.900
It’s better to submit the final cut if you
can.

